Maintaining this package
========================

Bun downloads a great deal while it builds, and one of its inputs is a copy of
itself. Neither is possible in a build environment, so several sources here are
generated rather than fetched by download_files. This file records how to
regenerate each of them.


What is being packaged
----------------------

1.4.0 is the first release written in Rust; 1.3.14 was the last written in
Zig. Source0 is the bun-v1.4.0 release tarball. %{git_commit} is the commit
that tag points at, passed as GIT_SHA so `bun --revision` reports it; it is
not part of the RPM version.


Regenerating the sources
------------------------

After changing Version, in order:

  1. osc service manualrun download_files      Source0, the Bun tarball
  2. ./bun_webkit                              Source1
  3. ./bun_prefetch                            Source2, Source3, Source4

The two scripts are ordinary generators run from this directory, not source
services: they need network access, a lot of disk and a working Bun, none of
which belong in a build. Both read their inputs out of the Bun tarball - the
dependency list, the pinned WebKit revision, the crate lockfile - so neither
has to be edited when a dependency is added, removed or re-pinned. Each finds
the tarball by itself; pass --source to override.

Commit the regenerated archives together with the new tarball. They are only
valid as a set: every one of them is pinned to the commit in Source0.

bun_prefetch needs `bun` on PATH (it runs `bun install`), plus cargo, curl,
openssl and Node.js. bun_webkit checks out about 6 GB before pruning, so point TMPDIR
somewhere with room - and at a real disk, not at /tmp if that is a tmpfs.


Source1 - WebKit
----------------

WebKit is Bun's JavaScript engine. It is the one dependency Bun's build system
does not fetch: scripts/build/deps/webkit.ts expects either a prebuilt engine
downloaded from upstream's releases, or a checkout made by hand. Building it
from source is much slower than taking the prebuilt engine, but it keeps the
package free of foreign binaries.

GitHub refuses to generate archives for a repository this large, so bun_webkit
clones the tree and prunes what a JavaScriptCore build never reads:

    LayoutTests PerformanceTests WebDriverTests ManualTests   test suites
    JSTests                                                   JSC's own tests
    Websites                                                  webkit.org content
    Tools/WebKitBot                                           Apple's CI bot

Together the test suites and website content are about five times the size of
everything the build uses.

Tools/WebKitBot is pruned for a different reason: it is a Node.js service -
Apple's commit/CI bot - whose npm lockfile pins packages with published CVEs
(browserslist 4.28.2, CVE-2026-73088, boo#1275410), and security scanners
report anything named in a shipped lockfile as a package embedded in bun. No
code from it is built or shipped - nothing in a JSCOnly build reads Tools/
beyond the build scripts - so dropping it removes the finding at the source.
The archive can be re-checked at any time with

    tar --zstd -tf webkit-*.tar.zst | grep -i browserslist

The revision is read out of the Bun tarball and written to webkit.revision
next to the archive, so a version bump cannot leave the engine behind.


Source2 - the C and C++ dependencies
------------------------------------

Bun's build downloads about twenty C and C++ dependencies. Upstream supports
running that offline: every download is looked up first in a content-addressed
cache, at

    $BUN_BUILD_PREFETCH_DIR/by-url/<sha256(url) truncated to 32 hex chars>

and copied from there on a hit (scripts/build/download.ts). Source2 is exactly
that layout, so the build system does not have to be patched to build offline.

The URLs are not hardcoded here. bun-deps.mjs imports Bun's own dependency
definitions out of the unpacked tree and asks each one for its source, so a
version bump picks up changes automatically. It runs under plain Node.js with
--experimental-strip-types - deliberately, so that regenerating the sources
does not itself need a Bun or a C++ toolchain.

bun-prefetch.manifest lists every entry as `key  sha256  url` and is committed
alongside, so the contents of the archive can be audited without unpacking it.


Source3 - the Rust crates
-------------------------

Plain `cargo vendor`, not obs-service-cargo: the workspace has path
dependencies on vendor/lolhtml and vendor/rust-argon2, which are downloads in
Source2, so cargo cannot even read the manifest until those have been
unpacked. bun_prefetch does the steps in the right order. rust-argon2 used to
be a crates.io crate; 1.4.0 vendors it to apply
patches/rust-argon2/legacy-low-memory.patch.

The crates unpack to cargo-vendor/ rather than vendor/, which in Bun's tree
already holds WebKit and lol-html; cargo requires every entry of its vendor
directory to be a crate and rejects the ones that are not. The [source]
configuration cargo prints is not kept - it records an absolute path from the
machine that ran the generator - so %prep writes its own into CARGO_HOME. It has
to go there rather than into .cargo/config.toml, which Bun's build regenerates
on every configure.


Source4 - the npm packages
--------------------------

Bun's code generators run `bun install` in three directories. The packages are
installed by bun_prefetch and unpacked in %prep, and bun-offline-build.patch
reduces the install steps to touching their ninja stamps, so nothing in the
build reaches the registry.

Patching the rule is what it takes. Pre-creating those stamp files from %build
does not work: on a fresh build directory ninja has no .ninja_log entry for any
edge, reports "command line not found in log" and runs every one of them
regardless of how up to date its outputs are.

The archive is architecture-neutral, which does not come for free. Several of
these packages - esbuild, oxlint, lightningcss - ship their real payload as a
prebuilt binary in a separate npm package per platform, listed as an optional
dependency and selected by CPU. `bun install` unpacks only the one matching the
machine it runs on, so an archive generated on aarch64 is missing exactly the
x86_64 binaries, and vice versa. bun_prefetch therefore downloads the packages
for every architecture in ExclusiveArch itself, reading the names, versions and
integrity hashes out of bun.lock - which needs curl, openssl and node besides
bun.

Those extra packages are placed in the top level node_modules rather than in
bun's store layout under node_modules/.bun. Every consumer reaches them through
require(), and the top level directory is the last place Node's resolution looks
walking up from anywhere below it, so one copy there is found from any depth
without reproducing bun's symlink farm. Each consumer is a launcher that picks
by process.arch at run time, so the architecture that is not being built is
never opened.

One thing does not choose at run time: node_modules/.bin/esbuild, which bun
links straight at the native binary rather than at the package's own launcher,
and which is the path the build invokes (scripts/build/configure.ts). %prep
repoints every .bin/esbuild at ../esbuild/bin/esbuild - the node script npm
links there - which then resolves the platform package normally.


Source100, Source101 - the bootstrap binaries
---------------------------------------------

Building Bun requires an existing Bun. Not to drive the build - that is a
TypeScript program which runs on plain Node, and upstream's own CI runs it that
way (.buildkite/ci.mjs), which is why %build invokes it with node rather than
with the bootstrap binary. But 24 of the ninja edges it generates do need Bun -
the code-generation steps. (Upstream has three `bun install` edges as well;
bun-offline-build.patch stamps those out.) The code generators use
Bun.build and Bun.Transpiler, and it is Bun's own bundler that emits the
JavaScript builtins with JavaScriptCore private-name intrinsics; esbuild cannot
substitute for it. Two further calls happen at configure time
(scripts/build/codegen.ts, bindgenv2 output listing).

So, as for ghc, rust and sbcl, the first build starts from an upstream binary.
Upstream tracks removing the dependency entirely (scripts/build/CLAUDE.md, "TODO
- remaining cfg.bun usage"); until then, turn %bcond bootstrap off once the
package is in the distribution and it will build with the Bun already there.

1.3.14 is old - it is the last Zig release, while the source here is the 1.4.0
Rust tree - but it does the job: the build applies no minimum Bun version, and
upstream's own CI bootstraps this tree with 1.3.13 (scripts/bootstrap.sh).
Keep 1.3.14 until bun is in the distribution, then turn %bcond bootstrap off
so the package builds with itself. Do not switch the bootstrap zips to 1.4.0
while this package is still the first 1.4.0 in the distro - that would be
another foreign binary for no gain.

The binaries are NoSource: uploaded to the build service but not shipped in the
src.rpm, and used during %build only - never installed.

    bun-v1.3.14/bun-linux-x64-baseline.zip
      a063908ae08b7852ca10939bbdc6ceed3ddabce8fb9402dce83d65d73b36e6c7
    bun-v1.3.14/bun-linux-aarch64.zip
      a27ffb63a8310375836e0d6f668ae17fa8d8d18b88c37c821c65331973a19a3b

Verify against
https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/releases/download/bun-v1.3.14/SHASUMS256.txt


The Rust toolchain
------------------

Bun pins a Rust nightly in rust-toolchain.toml and expects rustup to install
it. Every unstable feature it uses is present in the Rust that Factory ships;
only the nightly gate is missing. So %prep deletes the pin and %build sets
RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP=1, which opens that gate on the distribution compiler - the
same mechanism rustc's own bootstrap uses. No nightly toolchain is downloaded
or packaged.

If a future snapshot does need a genuine nightly, %bcond rust_nightly leaves
the pin in place; a nightly would then have to be provided some other way.


Patches
-------

bun-offline-build.patch stops release builds from rebuilding the Rust standard
library. Upstream rebuilds it to get -Ctarget-cpu into it and to feed
cross-language LTO, but doing so resolves std's own dependencies from crates.io,
which a build environment cannot reach. The distribution compiler's prebuilt
std is what the rest of the system links against anyway, and cross-language LTO
is off in this build. The patch also passes --offline to cargo (--locked is
upstream's own since 1.4.0), and reduces the `bun install` ninja rule to
touching its stamp - see "Source4 - the npm packages".

bun-unknown-lint.patch adds unknown_lints to two allow() attributes that name a
lint newer than the released compiler. The workspace denies warnings, so an
unknown lint name is fatal; allowing unknown_lints alongside it is correct on
both the released compiler and the nightly upstream pins.

bun-highway-scalable-sve.patch sets HWY_DISABLED_TARGETS so that Highway's
length-agnostic SVE targets are not compiled. Highway implements BitsFromMask
only for the fixed-length SVE targets - a uint64_t with one bit per lane is not
well-defined when the vector length is not known until run time - but
highway_xml.cpp, highway_json.cpp and highway_sourcemap.cpp call it
unconditionally, so all three fail to compile for HWY_SVE and HWY_SVE2. Those
targets become attainable on aarch64 whenever the compiler knows SVE and
Highway has runtime dispatch, which is every clang new enough to build bun.

What is left is NEON plus HWY_SVE_256 and HWY_SVE2_128, the fixed-length
targets that Neoverse V1, V2 and N2 select, and those do define BitsFromMask -
so no SVE hardware ends up on the NEON path. The define is applied both to
Highway's own translation units and to bun's, because HWY_TARGETS is derived
from it and the two disagreeing would misdispatch at run time.

Worth reporting upstream: it is a portability bug, not a packaging workaround.

bun-uniform-dwarf.patch makes the debug information something debugedit can
process, which rpm needs before it can extract a debuginfo package. Both of its
rustflags are there for that; the second only becomes necessary because of the
first.

It passes -Cdwarf-version=5 to rustc. Clang 21 and the
distribution's prebuilt Rust standard library both emit DWARF 5; rustc itself
still defaults to 4. Linking the two together produces one executable holding
.debug_ranges (the version 4 form) and .debug_rnglists plus .debug_str_offsets
(the version 5 form) at once, and debugedit cannot read that - it aborts with

    debugedit: tools/debugedit.c:1199: string_find_entry:
    Assertion `ret != NULL' failed.

which fails the build in %install, before rpm can extract anything. Going the
other way, -Cdwarf-version=4, does not help: the standard library is shipped
already compiled, so its version 5 sections stay whatever rustc is told.

Asking for version 5 is also what makes rustc emit .debug_names, the version 5
accelerator table, and debugedit has no support for that section at all:

    debugedit: .../usr/bin/bun: Unknown debugging section .debug_names

followed by the same assertion. So the patch turns it back off with
-Cllvm-args=-accel-tables=Disable. Nothing depends on the table - it is a name
index a debugger may use to avoid scanning .debug_info - and the alternative is
to generate a section the distribution's toolchain cannot process.

Both flags are rustc's, and only rustc's. Clang is not involved: 21 emits no
.debug_names under either debugger tuning, so upstream's -glldb
(scripts/build/flags.ts) is not the source, however much the section name
suggests LLDB. Checking the objects is what settles it - of the 1905 objects
and archives in a release build, the two that carry .debug_names are
libbun_rust.a and its deps/ copy.

bun-webkit-no-type-units.patch adds -fno-debug-types-section to the flags bun
passes to WebKit's cmake. It is the second thing that stopped rpm extracting a
debuginfo package, and it presents as the same assertion as above, with none of
the preceding messages - which is why it only became visible once the two
rustflags were in place.

Type units are comdat: the same type compiled into several translation units
produces the same unit in each object, and the linker keeps one copy and drops
the rest. Their strings are not comdat, so the .debug_str_offsets slots for the
strings that only a dropped unit named stay in the table with nothing pointing
at them. debugedit rewrites that table entry by entry, looking each old offset
up in the set it collected while walking the units, and asserts on the first
one it does not find. In an aarch64 release build 17 of the 13.4 million
entries are orphaned this way, all of them names of templates instantiated on a
lambda - the one case -gsimple-template-names cannot shorten, so the full name
with the build path in it is the only place those strings appear.

WebKit turns type units on itself, in Source/cmake/WebKitCompilerFlags.cmake,
and the comment there already says they break Linux distribution debuginfo
generation - it disables them for gcc for exactly that reason and leaves them
on for clang. WebKit's flags are prepended to CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS and bun's are
appended, so adding the negative form on bun's side is enough. The cost is
small: the type units are 14 MB of the 437 MB of .debug_info in a release
build.


Debuginfo and bun-profile
-------------------------

%install installs build/release/bun-profile, not build/release/bun. This is not
a debug build. A release build links bun-profile with full DWARF and then runs
the linker again with --strip-all --strip-debug --discard-all to produce bun
(scripts/build/flags.ts stripFlags, scripts/build/bun.ts emitPostLink /
emitStrip). The two are the same binary either side of that strip, so
installing bun leaves rpm nothing to extract: bun-debugsource comes out empty
and is deleted by the post-build check, and rpmlint then reports the shipped
file as unstripped anyway. rpm does its own stripping, so handing it the
unstripped one is both correct and what every other package does.


SQLite
------

bun:sqlite and node:sqlite are built against the SQLite amalgamation in bun's
own tree (src/jsc/bindings/sqlite/sqlite3.c), hence Provides: bundled(sqlite3).

This is not a free choice. bun's other mode, LAZY_LOAD_SQLITE=1, is written for
macOS: lazy_sqlite3.h dlopens a hardcoded "libsqlite3.dylib" and NodeSqlite.cpp
works around quirks specific to Apple's build. On Linux it compiles against the
system sqlite3.h and then finds nothing at run time. There is no configuration
in which bun links or loads the distribution's libsqlite3, so the bundled
amalgamation is the only working option; keep the version in the Provides in
step with SQLITE_VERSION in sqlite3_local.h on every bump.
